## UI Slice 2b-2 — Permission-aware editor lock

Makes the note editor honor the user's effective vault permission. A **read**-access collaborator now gets the note rendered but genuinely non-editable (no caret, no autosave); **edit**/**owner** keep the full editor from 2b-1.

This closes a real gap: after 2b-1, a read-only user got an editable-looking editor whose debounced saves silently 404'd server-side. The client now stops offering edits it knows the server will reject.

### What's in
- **`useCodeMirrorEditor`** gains a `readOnly?: boolean` option — applies CodeMirror's `EditorState.readOnly.of(true)` + `EditorView.editable.of(false)` (only when set). No new dependencies.
- **`canEdit(access)`** helper in `api/vaults.ts` — true only for `edit`/`owner`. Finally uses the `Vault.access` field that's been on the client since Slice 2a.
- **`NoteView`** derives `readOnly = !canEdit(vault?.access)`, passes it to the editor, guards the debounced save (`if (readOnly) return`), shows a subtle "· read-only" marker, and folds edit-capability into the remount key.

### Fail-safe by design
Unknown access (vault not yet loaded / query error) → **read-only**. The editor never falsely opens; the server is still the real gate (write routes guard `edit` and 404 below it) — this is the UX layer on top.

### Out of scope (later increments)
The editable frontmatter property panel (the `<dl>` stays read-only display — 2b-3), note create/rename/delete (2b-4), save-error surfacing, live-preview, wikilinks (2c).

